// alloc — package-level bump allocator over one qualified `Extent`
// (wiki/spec/resources/allocation.md, TASKS.md BUMP-ALLOCATOR-CANARY).
//
// The package owns only the allocation strategy: `bump.omg` carries the
// qualified-extent partition contract (`ExtentPartition`), the bump policy
// (`Bump`/`Attempt`), the monotonic `release` that retains its returned extent
// as retired content, the full-return `reset`, and the `BumpVec` reservation
// container with its two retired carriers: the finite `RetiredSlot` holding the
// older allocation a growth replaced, below the live buffer, and the freed
// suffix a truncation retired, above it. The compiler
// owns no allocator semantics here: every extent the strategy touches is
// `Extent in Granted & Vacant`, and custody is conserved through the boundary's
// authored `separate`/`embed` laws. Allocation borrows `Bump` briefly and
// returns only the owned extent. The remaining frontier is a `reset` that
// recomposes every retired extent rather than the two a caller can name,
// resident element lifecycle, and real backing.
//
// `vec.omg` adds the element-bearing customer: a growable `Vec<T>` over
// caller-supplied backing under the same explicit-supply / explicit-return
// contract, bounded at a fixed capacity ladder for this stage.
//
// The durable depend-edge consumer is
// `tests/omega/pass/memory/bump_allocator_canary`, with the vec surface
// exercised end-to-end by `tests/omega/pass/collections/runtime_vec_u32_growth_exit`.
use bump;
use vec;
